{
  "paraId": "1E47BEDF",
  "style": "",
  "text": "Notably, in this study HAIC was effective even though it used the same agents that had proved ineffective when given systemically. Consistent with this observation, HAIC with the same agents has also been reported to be effective in patients with CRC refractory to systemic chemotherapy 27, 28. We previously observed the same phenomenon in intrahepatic cholangiocarcinoma 22. Although the mechanism underlying this phenomenon remains unclear, a plausible explanation is that arterial delivery achieves concentrations of anticancer agents within the liver that are high enough to overcome resistance in tumor cells. In addition, only a small fraction of the administered drug reaches the systemic circulation, so exposure of non-hepatic tissues is limited 29. Although these regimens are suitable only for patients whose disease is confined mainly to the liver, they achieve high disease control rates, which translate into a survival benefit."
}

